> every number in that period will be visited over 10^10 calls to the RNG.

Oy gevalt.

First of all, you can't go through every integer in [0, MAX_RAND] in
MAX_RAND turns, because there's one more possible integer than there are
turns.  Second of all, consider the behaviour you're implying if MAX_RAND
is, say, 1.  If both 0 and 1 have to come up every MAX_RAND (+ 1) turns,
then you can predict what the next number will be.  This property applies
as MAX_RAND increases; basically, if a number comes up, you know for
certain it won't come up for at least MAX_RAND + 1 more rolls, and if
you've tracked the last few rolls you know what numbers are more likely
than others to come up in the next few.

A *good* prng does NOT have this property.  A good prng makes it very hard
to predict the next number based on a record of the numbers shown so far
in the sequence.

